
---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Banking Report ETL with Power Query | Eddie Zhu"
  description="Executive Dashboard project using Power BI, Power Query."
>

  <!-- PROJECT INTRODUCTION -->

  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">

      <a href="/projects" class="back-link">
        ← Back to Projects
      </a>

      <p class="eyebrow">PROCESS AUTOMATION</p>

      <h1>Banking Report ETL with Power Query</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        Excel Power Query ETL pipeline that imports, transforms, 
        and standardizes multiple simulated banking txt-based daily 
        reports into structured datasets for reporting and further analysis.
      </p>

      <div class="technologies">
        <span>Excel</span>
        <span>Power Query</span>
        <span>ETL</span>
      </div>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/banking-report-etl-powerquery"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        View GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- PROJECT OBJECTIVE -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>1. Project Objective</h2>

      <p>
        This project is a Power Query-based ETL pipeline designed to 
        simulate a real-world banking reporting system. It processes 
        multiple structured txt-based reports, transforms them into 
        clean Excel tables, and prepares them for further analysis in Excel.
      </p>

      <p>
        The solution demonstrates how Excel can be used as a lightweight data 
        engineering tool for batch report processing. Key steps are one parameter 
        change and one Refresh All instead of regular manual Text Import Wizard steps for every report.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>2. Business Problem and Why Power Query</h2>

      <p>
        Steps when importing data were repeated for seven reports each business day by hand. 
        The break lines and formats have to be set again each time and different staff placed 
        the break lines and formatted columns in various ways, which lacked in consistency.
      </p>

      <div class="method-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>The Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Operation admin downloaded seven daily reports from system and processed them using Excel for analysis. 
            Each report was prepared in the following way,
          </p>

          <ul>
          <li>Open the txt-based file in Excel.</li>
          <li>Text Import Wizard,</li>
          <ol>step 1: choose Fixed width</ol>
          <ol>step 2: click to place column break lines at the right character positions</ol>
          <ol>step 3: set the data format of each column</ol>
          <li>Finish.</li>
          <li>Delete the report header and footer by hand. Or re-format and/or calculate columns as required.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Why Power Query</h3>
          <p>
            The operations team has no access to SQL, Python or other data processing tools, 
            and uses Excel at a basic level. Built into Excel, Power Query makes the daily 
            import a single parameter change and one click of Refresh All, with no coding needed to run the process.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- IMPLEMENTATION -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>3. Technical Implementation</h2>
      
      <div class="implementation-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>Excel</h3>
          <p>
            Access to Power Query, store reports processed by Power Query.
          </p>
          
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Power Query</h3>
          <p>
            Set up parameter 'Path', identify rows by rules, process fixed-width columns, output processed reports to Excel sheet.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <article>
          <h3>Architecture</h3>
          <img
            src="/images/banking/architecture.png"
            alt="Technical architecture of the Chain Ladder reserving model"
            class="architecture-image"
           />

        </article>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- SOLUTION -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>4. The Solution</h2>

      <p>
        A pipeline by Power Query is created in one Excel workbook with following steps,
         to show how repetitive manual processing could be made more efficient and 
         consistent with the existing tool constrains:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Creates defined parameter Path to the folder for current working day, such as ~./14102026.</li>
        <li>Reads each report from the day's folder -- 14102026.</li>
        <li>Creates Target Rows Indicator for data rows, and drops header rows, footer rows and non-data rows.</li>
        <li>Splits the fixed-width lines of data into columns based on position, trims the text, applies appropriate data types and renames columns.</li>
        <li>Loads each report to its own sheet for further analysis.</li>
      </ul>

      <article>
          <h3>Comparison between Manual Steps and Power Query</h3>
          <img
            src="/images/banking/before_after.png"
            alt="before_after steps"
            class="before_after steps"
           />

        </article>

      <article>
          <h3>New Workflow</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>New daily reports are downloaded into a folder, eg 14102026.</li>
        <li>Folder path is set via Manage Parameters in Power Query.</li>
        <li>In Excel, Data -> "Refresh All” to launch data import process.</li>
        <li>All reports are loaded into Excel sheets automatically.</li>
      </ul>
        </article>

      <article>
          <h3>Files Inventory</h3>
          <img
            src="/images/banking/files_inventory.png"
            alt="files_inventory"
            class="files_inventory"
           />

        </article>

    </div>
  </section>



  <!-- LEARNING -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>5. Skills Developed</h2>

      <div class="skills-list">
        <span>Excel</span>
        <span>Power Query</span>
        <span>Data Analytics</span>
        <span>ETL</span>
        <span>Automation</span>
        <span>Reconciliation</span>
        <span>Problem Solving</span>
        <span>Efficiency</span>
        <span>Consistency</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- GITHUB -->

  <section class="project-contact">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>Explore the Project</h2>

      <p>
        View the Excel and Power Query model, detailed steps
        and technical documentation on GitHub.
      </p>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/banking-report-etl-powerquery"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        Visit GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>

</MainLayout>


<style>
  .project-hero {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    padding: 75px 0;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: #BFDBFE;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 35px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: #93C5FD;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
  }

  .project-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .subtitle {
    max-width: 750px;
    color: #DCE6F3;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .technologies,
  .skills-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 25px 0;
  }

  .technologies span,
  .skills-list span {
    background: #EFF6FF;
    color: #1D4ED8;
    padding: 7px 12px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .method-grid,
  .implementation-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 30px;
  }

  .method-grid article,
  .implementation-grid article {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 22px;
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
    font-size: 1.8rem;
  }

  h3 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
  }

  .alternate {
    background: #EDF2F8;
  }

  .section p,
  .section li {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .project-contact {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 65px 0;
  }

  .project-contact h2 {
    color: white;
  }

  .project-contact p {
    color: #DCE6F3;
  }

  @media (max-width: 650px) {
    .method-grid,
    .implementation-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
